Chitinlash
Ingredients: OLD SKOOL JUNGLE & DRUM 'N' BASS + CYBERPUNK & DYSTOPIAN + Ukulele
Creative Catalyst: It was first performed at a clandestine shotgun wedding uniting two warring cybernetic circus dynasties in an abandoned cooling tower.

Chitinlash is a hyper-caffeinated, rust-flecked collision where the frantic nylon rasgueado of Hawaiian ukulele is hijacked by 172-BPM old skool jungle Amen breaks and tectonic, dystopian Reese subs. It sounds like an acoustic island instrument possessed by an automated scrap-yard factory: lightning-fast four-string fingerpicking skitters over chopped breakbeats like furious insect mandibles, while sub-bass frequencies rattle the rib cages of cybernetically altered carnival runaways.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 168–174 BPM. A frantic, polyrhythmic breakcore-adjacent groove where syncopated sixteenth-note ukulele strumming interlocks directly with chopped Amen and Think breaks, producing a relentless, chittering rhythmic propulsion.
- Harmonic Palette: Open Hawaiian slack-key tunings forced into harsh Phrygian dominant and diminished scales, characterized by eerie minor-second clashes, dissonant drone notes, and sour, microtonally bent fifths.
- Production Signatures: Piezo contact mics glued directly to acoustic spruce bodies and routed through modular bitcrushers; extreme Akai S950 timestretching that stretches ukulele plucks into metallic, scraping insect screams; sub-basslines synthesized from the amplified, struck tension cables of high-wire circus rigging.
- Dynamics: Abrupt, heart-stopping dropouts where all rhythm vanishes into dry, intimate fingerpicking, immediately followed by concussive, low-end drops that detonate breakbeats and blown-out tape saturation in a claustrophobic wall of sound.

